.prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-mcp-household-agents.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/mcp-household-agents.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "The entry point's allowed_hosts wiring has no test through main()", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `arrt/src/arrt/__main__.py` now passes `allowed_hosts=settings.allowed_hosts` to `create_app`. No test checks that line. test_config.py tests `Settings.from_env` and test_host_guard.py calls `create_app(allowed_hosts=...)` directly, so deleting the kwarg in `main()` leaves every suite green. A deployment that set CURATION_ALLOWED_HOSTS would then get 421 on every browser and MCP request, and the refusal would tell the operator to set the setting they already set. The core learnings rule 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value' applies here. test_startup.py already has the pattern (`monkeypatch.setattr(entry_point, \"create_app\", capture)`, as in test_the_entry_point_asks_for_the_topic_sweep). Add a case that sets CURATION_ALLOWED_HOSTS=arrt.example, runs `entry_point.main()` and asserts the captured `allowed_hosts == frozenset({\"arrt.example\"})`. Watch it fail once with the kwarg removed.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/__main__.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Origin check admits any page served from an IP address, and the Host rationale does not cover Origin", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `HostGuard` runs `Origin` through `is_allowed`, which passes every IP literal. Allowing IPs is justified for `Host`: a rebinding page's Host is its own domain. It is not justified for `Origin`. A page at `http://203.0.113.5/`, or on any LAN device's address, sends `Origin: http://203.0.113.5`, passes the check, and can fire CORS-simple cross-origin requests at the server. Bodyless POSTs need no preflight: `/api/works/{id}/archive`, `/restore`, `/acquisition/retry`, `/api/themes/{id}/default`, Ask's `POST /threads`. The guard does refuse that same page when it is served from a domain, so the 'which pages may call it' promise in the module docstring and in api-contract.md \u00a7 Security holds only for domain-named pages. UUID ids limit how far this can be exploited today, hence WARNING. Fix: admit an `Origin` only when its name equals the request's own Host name, or is localhost or a listed name. Add a test case `Origin: http://10.9.9.9` with `Host: 127.0.0.1` that expects 403 (`http://127.0.0.1:8770` against Host 127.0.0.1 still passes). Update the Origin bullet in the records to match.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/hosts.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_host_guard.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint change-fragment: bad-type 'feat'", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Relayed from the manifest's record_lint: `.prawduct/changes/mcp-household-agents.md` frontmatter has `type: feat`, which is not one of feature, fix, chore, docs, refactor, perf, governance. Change it to `type: feature`.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/mcp-household-agents.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Origin check reuses the Host predicate, so a page served from any IP-literal origin passes it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: HostGuard runs `is_allowed(_origin_name(origin), allowed)` for Origin, the same test it runs for Host. The reason given for passing every IP address ('a rebinding page's requests carry its own domain name, never an address', hosts.py docstring and api-contract.md \u00a7 Security) holds for Host and does not hold for Origin. The Origin check exists to refuse a foreign page that calls the server by a name it accepts (test_a_foreign_page_is_refused_even_by_a_listed_name). A page hosted at `http://203.0.113.5/`, or on any compromised LAN device's web UI at its address, sends `Origin: http://203.0.113.5`, which passes as 'an IP address', and its no-preflight requests reach /api with an allowed Host. One example: a fetch with no-cors and an untyped Blob body carries no Content-Type, and FastAPI parses that body as JSON. Who goes wrong: the operator and the security model believe a page at any other origin is refused (403), and this one is not. Fix: an Origin passes only when its host is localhost or loopback, a listed name, or the same host as this request's own Host (a page served from the server's own address calling itself). Keep the any-IP rule for Host alone. Add a test case for `Origin: http://<some other IP>` with Host set to the server's address that expects 403, and watch it fail against the current predicate. Correctness should rate whether this is exploitable; it is filed here because the cause is the shared predicate.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/hosts.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_host_guard.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "create_app docstring says every route sits behind the guard; the Player's routes are exempt", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The new docstring paragraph in create_app opens 'Every route and mount sits behind the Host and Origin guard', and the code installs the guard with `exempt=player.router.routes`. hosts.py and the artifacts state the exemption correctly, so nothing reads this sentence. Make the claim relational ('every route and mount but the Player's, which `arrt.http.hosts` exempts'), or delete it and point to hosts.py. Prose, so no fix commit is owed on its own account.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/app.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Two normalizers decide whether a listed name and a requested name are equal", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. config._hostnames reduces each listed name with `rstrip('.').lower()`, and hosts.hostname reduces the request's Host with its own copy of the same steps. The guard is correct only while the two copies agree. If one changes (IDNA or punycode, whitespace) and the other does not, a listed name stops matching its own requests and nothing reports it. Today they agree, so this is borderline. Cheapest construction: have config._hostnames normalize through one shared function (or have HostGuard normalize `allowed_hosts` with `hostname` in __init__), so a single owner defines what 'the same name' means.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/config.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/hosts.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Origin check admits any IP-address origin on the Host half's reasoning, which does not hold for Origin", "recommendation": "The Host and Origin checks share one predicate, `is_allowed`, so any IP literal and `localhost` pass in `Origin` as well as in `Host`. The only reason recorded for that (hosts.py docstring, the plan's DECISION, api-contract \u00a7 Security: 'a rebinding page's requests cannot carry one: its Host is the domain it rebound') is about `Host`. It says nothing about `Origin`. A page served from any IP address (a public `http://203.0.113.9`, a LAN device's own web UI) or from another port on localhost sends an `Origin` the guard admits. That page can still send simple cross-origin requests that need no preflight. /api has several state-changing POSTs that take no body (`/works/{id}/archive`, `/works/{id}/restore`, `/themes/{id}/default`, `/works/{id}/acquisition/retry`). The 403 text ('A page at X may not call this server'), `.env.example` ('so is one from a page at any other name (403)') and the change fragment's consumer note ('a web page cannot take it over through your browser') all tell a reader that foreign pages are refused. Core learning 'When a comment justifies code by a constraint, or names what a guard excludes, check the constraint and what else the guard breaks' applies: the reason covers the case its author imagined (rebinding through Host) and not the other half the same predicate decides. Scope: instance. Either record why IP and localhost origins are admitted, and that the guard defends against DNS rebinding and not cross-site requests, in the decision and in the prose that promises more. Or give Origin its own test, such as a listed name or the request's own Host, with a case for an IP-origin page.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/hosts.py", ".env.example"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "#53's fork was picked by the builder on an inferred acceptance, yet the records call it DECIDED", "recommendation": "The plan's Goals say the owner's 'let's keep going' is read as acceptance. They also say 'Inferred, not ruled: the owner had not seen the #53 finding below when they replied.' #53 says plainly that 'the decision is a fork and it needs an operator to pick a side, not an engineer to pick a default'. Its first acceptance criterion is 'An operator decision exists, dated'. Even so, `api-contract.md` \u00a7 Security headlines it 'DECIDED 2026-11-08', and `agents-and-memory.md` says the scope was 'accepted by the owner'. The choice has an operator-visible cost. Any deployment reached by name, which `security-model.md` says the reference one is (a `.lan` name through the reverse proxy), loses its browser UI and MCP on upgrade until `CURATION_ALLOWED_HOSTS` is set. Consequence: a maintainer or the PR reviewer reads the fork as owner-ratified and treats #53 as closeable, and the deploy goes ahead on a choice the owner never saw. Scope: instance. Before merge, put fork 1 against fork 2 and the upgrade cost in front of the owner, and record the ruling, or leave the records saying the decision is proposed rather than decided until they rule.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-mcp-household-agents.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/agents-and-memory.md"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records six mid-build decisions. (1) `brief` answers with no `count`, settled by a learnings rule. This changes Chunk 01's deliverable, which still reads 'with the count of judgments it covers'. (2) The guard is installed with `add_middleware` so it wraps the mounts and the static files. (3) `Origin` is parsed by hand because `test_persistence_boundary` keeps `urllib` out. (4) `CURATION_ALLOWED_HOSTS` refuses a URL, port or path at startup. (5) The setting is documented in `.env.example`, `deploy/README.md` and the compose example rather than in the operational spec. The plan says this one was 'Not settled by the goals', because Chunk 02's deliverables named the operational spec, so it is an alignment-pass miss for the owner to confirm. (6) Serving moved into `tests/serving.py`. Also: Chunk 02 said the Player's routes would be exempt by `contract/routes.json`'s list, and the build reads them from `player.router.routes` instead (api-contract says those are the same routes). Not settling the alignment-pass item also leaves the plan's own Chunk 02 bullet 'The operational spec gets the setting' unmet as written.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-mcp-household-agents.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/arrt#53", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Backlog item appears resolved: 'security: /mcp and /api check no Host or Origin header' (brookstalley/arrt#53; #20 and #34 redirect to it, #21 is already shipped). The guard covers /mcp and /api, and the dated decision in api-contract \u00a7 Security names `art_discovery`'s spend. Its first acceptance criterion asks for an operator decision, which is the open question in the finding above. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/arrt#53 status=shipped closed-by=scope:mcp-household-agents` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. No other open item's `affected:` paths cover the changed files, and security has no other open item (C-B3 clean). #53's own body lists a related id, brookstalley/arrt#187, that the cache cannot resolve. This diff does not cite it.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: core.md and surface.md read, one rule fired", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `prawduct-hook learnings-files --for-diff`: `.claude/rules/learnings/core.md` and `.claude/rules/learnings/surface.md`. 'When a comment justifies code by a constraint, or names what a guard excludes...' (core) fired, and the Origin finding above cites it. Surface rules checked against the change, with no regression found: 'Before a UI or notice says do X to fix this, grep for the endpoint and control...' (the refusals name `CURATION_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, which exists and is read at startup), and 'Before advertising an action, build a real call using only shipped surfaces...' (the `brief` tips point to `list`'s `affinity_id` for `delete`, which `_affinity_fields` returns). This cycle added or changed no learnings rule. No implicit supersession seen.", "fid": "R-11"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 5, "note": 5}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": "mcp-household-agents", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "6b22b48c41581df593d33eda3dd698d464a57f1b", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/agents-and-memory.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-mcp-household-agents.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/mcp-household-agents.md", "deploy/README.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "pla